4. Root Cause Diagnosis & Alert Rule
Root Cause Diagnosis

The OrderingMicroservice experienced checkout failures due to SQL database execution timeouts and payment gateway HTTP 504 errors.

SQL Database Failure:

Affected Operations: op-8921-az, op-8922-az
Exception: System.Data.SqlClient.SqlException
Method: OrderRepository.SaveOrderAsync
Root Cause: SQL database execution timeout.
Impact: HTTP 500 checkout failures.

Payment Gateway Failure:

Affected Operation: op-8925-az
Exception: System.Net.Http.HttpRequestException
Method: PaymentGatewayClient.AuthorizeCharge
Root Cause: Payment gateway timeout.
Impact: HTTP 504 checkout failure.
Recommended Remediation
Optimize SQL queries and stored procedures.
Investigate database execution timeouts.
Implement retry mechanisms with exponential backoff.
Monitor payment gateway response times.
Configure Azure Monitor alerts.
Monitor dependency failures using Application Insights.
Recommended Azure Monitor Alert Rule

Alert Name: OrderingMicroservice-Checkout-Failure-Alert

KQL Query:

requests
 | where cloud_RoleName == "OrderingMicroservice"
 | where success == false
 | summarize FailedRequests = count() by bin(timestamp, 5m)
 | where FailedRequests >= 2

Alert Configuration:

Evaluation Frequency: 5 minutes
Lookback Period: 5 minutes
Threshold: 2 or more failed requests
Severity: 2 (Warning)
Action: Notify operations team.
5. Conclusion

The investigation identified SQL database execution timeouts and payment gateway HTTP 504 errors as the primary causes of checkout failures.

The SQL dependency recorded a 100% failure rate across two observed calls, while Cosmos DB and Redis operated successfully.

Recommended improvements include SQL optimization, retry mechanisms, dependency monitoring, and Azure Monitor alerting.
